# Build o2-gpu-test for every AliceO2 PR, on the standard slc10 builder image.
#
# This check lives in the slc10/ directory rather than a slc10-gpu/ one on
# purpose, so the existing slc10 workers pick it up with no new pool: they list
# every *.env in their container's directory.
#
# CONTAINER_IMAGE is deliberately NOT overridden any more, so DEFAULTS.env's
# stock slc10-builder applies. ALIBUILD_O2_FORCE_GPU=build (added by
# alidist#6302) makes gpu-system.sh take CUDA and ROCm from alidist recipes
# instead of from whatever the image happens to ship, which is what removes the
# need for slc10-gpu-builder -- and with it the standing obligation to keep two
# images in step. The ARCHITECTURE was always slc10_x86-64 either way: the
# entrypoint derives it as ${CUR_CONTAINER%%-*}_$(uname -m), stripping at the
# FIRST dash, so the two images only ever differed in content, not target.

# ALIBUILD_O2_FORCE_GPU=build breaks out of gpu-system.sh's detection before it
# looks at anything, so nothing here depends on probing the host. The two
# architectures therefore have to be stated rather than discovered, and they are
# not just compiler inputs: gpu-system resolves to
# build-from-alidist-cudaarch@120@-rocmarch@gfx906@, so the arch lands in the
# package version and keeps these tarballs distinct.
#
# build-loop.sh forwards all three with -e. aliBuild builds the container
# environment from scratch, so exporting them here would not otherwise reach
# prefer_system_check, which is where gpu-system.sh reads them.
ALIBUILD_O2_FORCE_GPU=build
ALIBUILD_O2_FORCE_GPU_CUDA_ARCH="120"
ALIBUILD_O2_FORCE_GPU_HIP_ARCH="gfx906"

# The GPU is still passed through, and still needed -- not for detection, which
# no longer runs, but because O2-GPU-test exercises the backends it builds and
# wants real hardware to do it on. Repeats DEFAULTS.env, which this overrides
# wholesale.
DOCKER_EXTRA_ARGS='--tmpfs=/dev/shm:rw,size=10g,mode=1777 --cap-add=PERFMON --cap-add=IPC_LOCK --device amd.com/gpu=all --device nvidia.com/gpu=all'

# This check needs GPU hardware, so only workers running in the cluster's "gpu"
# node pool may claim it. claim-builder.sh treats this as a hard filter -- a
# worker elsewhere skips the row rather than taking work it cannot do -- and as a
# sort key, so a GPU worker drains GPU-only rows before work any worker could
# take. Otherwise the scarce machine spends its time on ordinary builds while
# this queue starves.
#
# Nomad's node_pool is a JOB-level field, so serving both pools means two jobs
# sharing this one queue, not one job spanning them. Checks are deliberately not
# split by directory: a GPU worker should still help with ordinary slc10 builds
# when the GPU queue is empty, and being able to is the point of claims.
REQUIRES_POOL=gpu

# And, on that pool, it goes first. REQUIRES_POOL already stops other workers
# taking this row, but it does not stop the GPU worker taking someone else's:
# with only staleness to order them, the one machine that can build this spent
# its rounds on alidist-dataflow -- work any of a dozen builders could have
# done -- while this queue waited. POOL_PRIORITY is the preference half of the
# pair, and it outranks staleness, so on the gpu pool this is claimed ahead of
# ordinary slc10 work rather than merely instead of it elsewhere.
#
# Only the gpu pool is listed, so this reorders nothing anywhere else: a worker
# in another pool scores this check 0, exactly as if the line were absent.
POOL_PRIORITY="gpu=10"

# JOBS, because this check does NOT run where slc10/DEFAULTS.env assumes it
# does. That file argues JOBS=24 from the ci-linux-x86 allocation -- 24 cores and
# 108 GiB -- and the number is right there. The GPU builder is a different shape:
# ci-linux-x86-gpu reserves 12 cores and 55296 MiB on aligpuci, which has only
# 62 GiB in total against 96000 MHz of CPU. It is CPU-rich and memory-poor, so
# JOBS here has to be bound by memory, not by cores.
#
# At 24 the sums do not work: build-loop.sh caps the container at
# NOMAD_MEMORY_LIMIT - 4096, so ~50 GiB shared 24 ways is ~2.1 GiB per compile.
# ONNXRuntime's flash-attention kernels need several GB each in nvcc's frontend,
# and the build died exactly there -- "nvcc error : cicc died due to signal 9",
# which is the OOM killer, not a compile error. Our own CUDA_ARCH=120 adds to it:
# the failing command carries -DCOMPILE_BLACKWELL_SM120_TMA_GROUPED_GEMMS, so
# Blackwell TMA GEMMs are built on top of the sm80 kernels.
#
# 12 = the cores this job actually reserves, which is the rule DEFAULTS.env makes
# the case for, and ~4.3 GiB per compile. That is not generous for these kernels;
# if cicc is killed again, lower this to 8 (~6.4 GiB) rather than raising the
# reservation -- the node has 55 of its 62 GiB already allocated, so there is
# nothing to raise it with.
#
# o2-alidist and o2-alidist-dataflow run on this same worker still at 24. They
# survive because nothing in them is as memory-hungry as flash-attention, but
# they are over-subscribed too, and JOBS is a plain assignment that the jobspec
# deliberately cannot override.
JOBS=12

CI_NAME=build_O2_gpu_test_slc10_x86
CHECK_NAME=build/O2/gpu-test-slc10-x86

# Every O2 PR, not alidist ones: this check exists to catch GPU breakage in O2
# itself. alidist comes along as a development package so a PR is tested against
# the recipes as they are now.
PR_REPO=AliceO2Group/AliceO2
# PR_REPO_CHECKOUT, and why its absence made this check meaningless.
#
# build-loop.sh defaults it to $(basename "$PR_REPO"), i.e. "AliceO2", and that
# is the directory it fetches the PR into and merges the PR commit in. aliBuild
# then builds whatever DEVEL_PKGS names -- which was the checkout "O2". Two
# different directories: the PR was merged into one and the other was compiled,
# so every verdict this check ever posted was about dev, not about the pull
# request it was reporting on.
#
# Setting it makes the merge target and the development package the same tree.
PR_REPO_CHECKOUT=O2
PR_BRANCH=dev
# O2-GPU-test, not o2-gpu-test: aliBuild keys on the `package:` field inside the
# recipe, not on the filename, and alidist/o2-gpu-test.sh declares
# "package: O2-GPU-test". Getting this wrong fails in aliBuild doctor -- "ERROR:
# o2-gpu-test should be spelt O2-GPU-test" -- which the check reports as the
# generic "aliBuild doctor error", giving no hint that the cause is a typo in
# this file.
PACKAGE=O2-GPU-test
ALIBUILD_DEFAULTS=o2
# NOT pinned to an alidist PR right now, which is the correct steady state: the
# check builds alidist master, so it picks up every recipe fix as it lands.
#
# Pinning here is legitimate and occasionally necessary -- this is the only place
# gpu-system.sh is built with the GPU actually enabled, so a recipe change there
# has nowhere else to be proven -- but it is a DEBT, not a setting. Add a line
#
#     alisw/alidist pull/<N>/head
#
# above the $PR_REPO line, and UNPIN IT THE DAY <N> MERGES. refs/pull/N/head keeps
# resolving after a merge, so a stale pin does NOT fail: the check just keeps
# building a frozen snapshot of alidist, silently missing every recipe fix made
# since. There is no alarm for this. It has already happened twice.
#
# Pin history:
#   alidist#6193 (ONNXRuntime 1.29.0)          merged 2026-08-25, unpinned then.
#     While that one was stale it hid the gettext fix in #6313 for weeks.
#   alidist#6302 (Add build option to gpu-system.sh)
#     merged 2026-09-05 13:16 UTC as ce9d49caaab1, unpinned the same day.
DEVEL_PKGS="$PR_REPO $PR_BRANCH $PR_REPO_CHECKOUT"

TRUST_COLLABORATORS=true
DONT_USE_COMMENTS=1

# Repeated from o2-alidist.env rather than inherited: *.env files do not inherit
# from each other, only from the DEFAULTS.env chain. If a third slc10 check
# appears, move these two into mesosci/slc10/DEFAULTS.env instead of copying
# them again.
#
# aliBuild 2.0, which is what slc10 is being brought up against, and the CAS
# store, which is the only one carrying slc10_x86-64 packages.
INSTALL_ALIBUILD='alisw/alibuild@refs/pull/1038/head'
REMOTE_STORE=https://s3.cern.ch/swift/v1/alibuild-repo
